1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,332
Every one of bob's 68,096 configuration bits lives in one memory.

2
00:00:05,652 --> 00:00:11,873
This chapter is about that memory, the simplest way to fill it, and the sequence that starts a design.

3
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:18,806
The configuration memory is organised as AMD organises its own: in frames.

4
00:00:19,126 --> 00:00:26,328
A frame is four 32-bit words, 128 bits, and the memory is 532 of them.

5
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:32,185
Frames go column by column, like the configuration columns of a seven-series part.

6
00:00:32,505 --> 00:00:34,782
Column zero is the control tile.

7
00:00:35,102 --> 00:00:42,927
Then each grid column in turn, its tiles from bottom to top, each tile's block fields followed by its routing multiplexers.

8
00:00:43,247 --> 00:00:49,009
A logic-block column needs fifty-one frames; the memory and multiplier columns far fewer.

9
00:00:50,567 --> 00:00:53,509
How do bits get from JTAG into that memory?

10
00:00:53,829 --> 00:00:58,076
The first obvious answer writes a frame with a computed index into the memory.

11
00:00:58,396 --> 00:01:03,864
Synthesis turns that into a shifter across every bit, and the host tools ran out of memory.

12
00:01:04,333 --> 00:01:11,369
The second obvious answer, the textbook scan chain, keeps a whole second copy of the memory as a shift register.

13
00:01:11,689 --> 00:01:14,119
It works, but it doubles the cost.

14
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:22,706
bob's answer is to stream everything through a single frame buffer of one hundred and twenty-eight bits, and write each completed frame into place.

15
00:01:23,026 --> 00:01:25,613
That one change paid for the larger grids.

16
00:01:27,167 --> 00:01:29,382
The first way in is the scan chain.

17
00:01:29,702 --> 00:01:38,758
Select the private instruction chain in, and shift all 68,096 bits through TDI in one scan, least significant first.

18
00:01:39,078 --> 00:01:40,585
They enter the frame buffer.

19
00:01:41,067 --> 00:01:49,975
Every hundred and twenty-eight bits complete a frame, which is written into its place in memory on the next falling clock edge, and the frame counter moves on.

20
00:01:50,295 --> 00:01:56,438
But only while writes to a running design are disabled: a running design is never rewritten underneath itself.

21
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:01,117
Reading the chain back works the same way in reverse, and never writes.

22
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:10,736
While the bits stream in, a CRC is computed one bit at a time, with the Castagnoli polynomial, and a counter counts them.

23
00:02:11,056 --> 00:02:19,337
At the end of the scan the design is committed only if the count is exactly the chain width and the CRC equals the value the host promised in advance.

24
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:21,063
Two details.

25
00:02:21,383 --> 00:02:27,688
The CRC starts at all ones, so a TDI wire stuck at zero cannot accidentally match.

26
00:02:28,008 --> 00:02:35,337
And the length check is guarded by a mutation test, because the first version of it could be deleted with every test still passing.

27
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:39,533
The chain has a control register beside it.

28
00:02:39,853 --> 00:02:49,121
Reading it returns the last CRC, how many bits arrived, the error flags, whether a configuration is committed, and the four startup signals.

29
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:54,734
Writing it sets the expected CRC, but only if the top byte carries a key.

30
00:02:55,054 --> 00:03:04,066
Reading a JTAG register shifts new bits in at the same time, so without the key, every status read would silently overwrite the expected value.

31
00:03:05,633 --> 00:03:08,469
A loaded design does not start by itself.

32
00:03:08,789 --> 00:03:16,645
After power-up or JPROGRAM, global set-reset and the output tristate are held, writes are disabled, and DONE is low.

33
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:23,012
With the JSTART instruction loaded, each test clock in Run-Test Idle advances one phase.

34
00:03:23,332 --> 00:03:27,497
First the flip-flops leave reset, taking their initial values.

35
00:03:27,817 --> 00:03:29,582
Then the outputs are enabled.

36
00:03:29,902 --> 00:03:33,431
Then writes are enabled, so the design starts to run.

37
00:03:33,751 --> 00:03:35,670
Last, DONE rises.

38
00:03:36,133 --> 00:03:47,660
This follows UG470's principle, check first and then release in a safe order, but the order is bob's own: DONE comes last, so a lit DONE LED means the design is really running.

39
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:51,350
And nothing advances unless a configuration was committed.

40
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:54,981
JPROGRAM resets all of it.

41
00:03:55,301 --> 00:04:04,438
The configuration flip-flops clear, every CFGLUT5 is swept to zero, readback is invalidated, and startup returns to phase zero.

42
00:04:04,758 --> 00:04:09,196
What survives is the expected CRC and the contents of the block RAMs.

43
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:14,709
Every mux now selects zero and every table is zero: the dark fabric again.

44
00:04:15,029 --> 00:04:20,448
That is also why a load straight after JPROGRAM may skip every frame that is all zeros.

45
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,210
One more saving.

46
00:04:23,530 --> 00:04:29,663
Every frame written into the memory is also written, on the same edge, into a block RAM of the host.

47
00:04:29,983 --> 00:04:33,364
Readback, on either path, reads that copy.

48
00:04:33,833 --> 00:04:41,243
Before this, readback was a giant multiplexer over the configuration flip-flops, about a third of the whole design.

49
00:04:41,563 --> 00:04:48,829
The honest trade-off is that readback now proves what was written, not what the cells hold; the capture checks cover the cells.

50
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:55,671
The memory, the chain control and the startup sequence are three small Verilog files.

51
00:04:55,991 --> 00:05:04,557
Their testbenches take every expected value from the Python models, and a mutation suite breaks each guard in turn to prove the tests notice.

